Credits. No code from the projects below was copied; CoreCleaner is an independent
implementation. What they gave us are ideas and a map of where things live.

* luchasarie/agent-cleanup (MIT), https://github.com/luchasarie/agent-cleanup
  The idea of a cleaner for the traces coding agents leave behind, and its
  survey of the folders Claude Code and Codex fill up. CoreCleaner re-derived the
  folder list as data (skills/corecleaner/references/categorias.md) and checked it
  against the vendors' documentation.

* daymade/claude-code-skills, macos-cleaner (MIT),
  https://github.com/daymade/claude-code-skills
  The macOS lessons that sizes lie on APFS (clones, hardlinks, local snapshots),
  that the Trash is the safe exit, and that a git bundle is the way to archive a
  repo before removing it.

Run time dependency, downloaded only with the user's consent and only after a
sha256 check against skills/corecleaner/references/pins.tsv:

* dua-cli by Byron (MIT), https://github.com/Byron/dua-cli
  Not bundled with CoreCleaner and not linked into it; it is run as a separate
  program, and without it CoreCleaner falls back to a slower built-in scan.
